WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.428 --> 00:00:16.720
GRAYSON HOWARD: Good afternoon.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.940
My name is Grayson Howard.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.580
I'm a sophomore English major
here at Hillsdale College,

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.520
and I will be introducing
our speaker today.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.160
Christopher Caldwell is
a contributing editor

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.120
at the Claremont Review of
Books and a contributing opinion

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.610
writer for The New York Times.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.630
A graduate of Harvard
College, his essays, columns,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.810
and reviews appear in The Wall
Street Journal, The New York

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:44.100
Times Book Review, The
Spectator, Financial Times,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.050
the Claremont
Review of Books, and

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.940
numerous other publications.

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.010
He is the author of reflections
on the revolution in Europe,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.010
immigration, Islam,
and the West,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.560
and The Age of Entitlement--

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.270
America Since the '60s,
forthcoming January, 2020.

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:04.860
Please welcome
Christopher Caldwell.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.318
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:13.945 --> 00:01:15.070
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL: Gosh.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:17.320
Well, thank you, Grayson.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.610
I would like to thank everyone
at Hillsdale for coming,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:25.960
but particularly the people
from the Center for Constructive

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.320
Alternatives, Matt Bell.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:34.810
I want to thank Kim Ellsworth
for organizing things.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.240
And so I guess that
what I'm doing here

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.340
is trying to figure out with
you whether Swedish socialism

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.515
constitutes a
constructive alternative.

00:01:44.515 --> 00:01:47.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.100
Maybe we can start by saying
there are two basic ways,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.630
I think, to think
about socialism.

00:01:55.630 --> 00:02:01.780
The first is as a doctrine
related to communism.

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.510
And among followers
of Karl Marx,

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:11.650
socialism is often just
another word for communism--

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.440
you talk about the Union of
Soviet Socialist Republics.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:19.360
But more usually, socialism
reflects an outcome

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.330
after Marxist
revolutionaries have

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:26.380
had to make compromises
or accommodate reality.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.900
It's the sort of government--
a socialist government--

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:33.100
you get when
revolutionaries are thwarted

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:37.150
by entrenched
powers of some kind,

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:41.200
or by the weakness of their
own revolutionary leaders,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.900
or by popular dislike of it.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.930
Socialism described this
way tends to be unpopular.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.860
So those who promote
socialism tend

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.780
to look at it in the second way.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.810
The second way to
look at socialism

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:05.350
is as its own separate
thing that has

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.320
nothing to do with communism.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.170
And in this
understanding, socialism

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:17.500
is just carrying out some old
and time-honored human impulse,

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:22.570
such as charity, or the golden
rule, or Christian ethics,

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:28.160
except in an up-to-date way that
adjusts to modern conditions.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.020
And this is the kind
of socialism that's

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.210
the more popular of the two.

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:38.110
And I think that this
is the kind of socialism

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:42.820
that today's millennials,
or young people,

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.820
are talking about when
they identify themselves

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.960
as socialists in such
strikingly high numbers.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.280
I think many of us have seen
the poll that was carried out

00:03:54.280 --> 00:04:01.600
by Reason magazine in 2014 that
showed that 42% of millennials

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.930
call themselves socialists or
are happy to call themselves

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.430
socialists.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.820
I think I'm less surprised
or alarmed by that poll

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.200
than many people.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.490
If you really look at it
very closely, only 16%

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.730
of these so-called
socialists realize

00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:22.870
that socialism means
government ownership

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:24.520
of the means of production.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:29.470
64% of these socialists
say they would

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:33.650
prefer that the economy be
run on a free-market basis.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:38.060
And 55% of them aspire to
start their own businesses.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.356
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:47.630
So I think that kids like this--

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:50.950
they would not choose to live in
the Soviet Union of the 1950s,

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:54.820
or the Albania of the 1960s.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.750
But the problem is,
while they're not

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.300
ready to fight for
socialism, they

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.730
might be convinced to
vote for people who

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:05.720
want to fight for socialism.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.080
They seem to want a nicer
version of our own system

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:15.010
which, in itself, is not
an indefensible desire.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:19.840
And they've lately taken to
calling that desire socialism.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.100
And that's why people
like Bernie Sanders

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.210
point to Sweden--

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:28.840
and particularly Sweden
in, say, the 1970s--

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:29.800
as a model.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:36.370
And certainly, it
is a real challenge.

00:05:36.370 --> 00:05:39.640
You can say that
ABBA and Bjorn Borg

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:44.020
are more attractive figures
than Marx and Engels, right?

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:47.590
But I think the argument
is about more than that.

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:50.470
Sweden, in the 1970s,
actually seemed

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:56.080
to square a lot of circles
in terms of policy.

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.730
It was, at the time, the
most egalitarian country

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:01.930
in Western Europe.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.560
But it was also the fourth
richest country per capita

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:06.850
in the world.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:12.785
It had very high, almost total
working-class union membership.

00:06:12.785 --> 00:06:17.020
But at the same time, it had
perhaps Europe's best developed

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:19.820
culture of entrepreneurship.

00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:22.540
So there would seem to
be things worth studying.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:24.800
And a lot of people
back then said, well,

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.090
we could do that, if
only we could figure out

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:32.530
what this Swedish model was
and follow it ourselves.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:34.480
And that leads to
the two questions

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.020
that will preoccupy me
for the next half an hour,

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:42.010
say, which is, what
is this Swedish model?

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:45.762
And why do we think that
it can be replicated?

00:06:45.762 --> 00:06:48.220
So the things I'm going to talk
about in the next half hour

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.740
are, first, the origins
of Swedish socialism

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:57.310
in the 19th century, its
relationship to Marxism,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.820
its early development
up into the 1930s.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:07.060
Then the golden age
of Swedish socialism--

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.460
after the '30s, but
especially after the '60s,

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:14.980
and into the 1980s, which showed
these surprising strengths

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:18.490
that I talk about,
like an ability

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.340
to coexist with
entrepreneurship.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:25.450
Then its decline in the 1990s.

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:28.580
And then a new twist
on Swedish socialism--

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:33.250
I would like to
ask, to what degree

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:38.050
is it responsible for
Sweden's immigration problem?

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:40.940
Because I do think that, when
you think of Sweden today,

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.030
the one thing you
must understand

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:48.430
is that it has a uniquely
disruptive immigration problem,

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:51.010
even by European standards.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.110
And then finally, I will
get to this question

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:57.440
of whether the Swedish model
is viable, even for Sweden.

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:03.320
And if so, whether it is
replicable for the rest of us.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:08.020
Now, if there's any
place in the world where

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:13.240
socialism ought to work the way
Bernie Sanders describes it--

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.030
that is, where it ought to be
thought of as ancient wisdom,

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:18.190
rather than Marxist ideology--

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:19.330
it's Sweden.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:25.690
I mean, if you go back to
the beginning, Sweden had--

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:31.820
unlike other places in Europe--
it had no contact with Rome.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:37.059
It was a total stranger to
the complex, differentiated,

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:40.780
articulated society that
Roman conquest always

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:42.909
brought in its wake.

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:47.740
I mean, there are primitive
places all over Europe.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:49.870
But Sweden didn't
even have any contact

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:53.420
with this complex society.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:57.380
It didn't even have feudalism.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:58.400
It was primitive.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:01.580
It was uncultured.

00:09:01.580 --> 00:09:05.630
Its literature figures
in the most marginal way

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.990
in this great blossoming
of European literature

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:13.880
that covers almost every
European country from Germany,

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:21.080
to France, to Spain, to Italy,
to Poland, to the Netherlands.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:22.670
And it was really poor.

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:30.920
So in the late 19th century,
20% of the population of Sweden

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:31.550
immigrated.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.940
And since much of it is
to this part of the world,

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:38.508
there are probably many
children of that immigration

00:09:38.508 --> 00:09:39.050
in this room.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:41.690
So I don't have to
explain that to you.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:47.720
But there were upsides to
this being downtrodden.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:53.010
Sweden had a strong
idea of equality.

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:59.120
It had a powerful idea of
social solidarity and community

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:01.700
of the sort that you
might associate only

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:07.190
with military
cultures like Sparta.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:09.390
The historian Sheri Berman--

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:11.780
who was, in general,
highly sympathetic

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:14.540
to Swedish socialism--

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:18.170
she notes the way that, even
in the late 19th century,

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:22.490
Sweden was neither
industrial nor democratic.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:26.550
And what that means is that
the Swedish Workers' Party--

00:10:26.550 --> 00:10:31.010
which is the forerunner
to the party that created

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:33.530
the Swedish socialist system--

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.450
they could claim to
have really brought

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:41.510
about industrialism
and universal suffrage

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:45.260
in a unique way.

00:10:45.260 --> 00:10:49.070
By the time most socialist
parties appear on the scene,

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:52.160
that system is already
all set up so that most

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:55.490
socialist parties are
basically just divvying up

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:58.820
the spoils in a way that
is more favorable to them.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:01.700
The Swedish Socialist
Party was really closer

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.920
to the roots of Swedish society.

00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:09.800
Nonetheless, as
people became aware

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:16.010
in the 1920s and the 1930s
what the Russian Revolution was

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:18.950
actually doing,
socialists in Sweden

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:21.680
aroused the same
kind of distrust

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:24.200
that they did everywhere else.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.170
They were attacked
as secret communists.

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:32.570
They were accused of caring more
about Swedes of certain classes

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:36.110
than they were about defending
Swedes against foreigners.

00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:39.830
And in the 1928 elections,
the Swedish socialists

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:43.430
were just wiped
out, which is what

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:45.170
was happening to
socialist parties

00:11:45.170 --> 00:11:47.510
all over Europe at the time.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:51.680
But the Swedes went back
to the drawing board.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:57.590
And in 1932, they campaigned
on a very different idea.

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:00.020
They campaigned
that Sweden ought

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:05.420
to be what they called a
folkhemmet, or people's home.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:09.630
They even called this
national socialism.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.660
And I don't say
this to accuse them

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:16.190
of being Nazis, really, as some
of their political opinions do.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:19.760
But we should know
that, in Sweden today,

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.930
when the historians we might
refer to as politically

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:28.460
correct talk about
socialism's early years,

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:33.650
they find it embarrassing
and a little bit creepy.

00:12:33.650 --> 00:12:38.210
What I will say is that
Swedish socialists were not

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:42.410
like their contemporaries
in France and Spain.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:45.590
In France and Spain,
socialists really

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:49.970
were trying to create
an international world

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:52.700
working-class solidarity.

00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:55.790
What Sweden was doing
was a lot more similar

00:12:55.790 --> 00:13:00.072
to what FDR was doing
in this country.

00:13:00.072 --> 00:13:02.450
If it was a socialism,
it was a socialism

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:07.310
that was tied to a great
deal of national feeling,

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.540
and even nationalism.

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:10.490
And that's why--

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:12.860
I think that
nationalism is why--

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:17.540
the Swedish socialists were the
only European socialist party

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:21.980
to survive the 1930s in power.

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:25.400
And they were aided in
that by having some really

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:27.590
extraordinary leaders.

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:30.410
So that began what
we can call, say,

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:35.710
the golden age of
Swedish socialism.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:37.220
And it's an interesting thing.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:40.610
With only a couple
of minor hiccups,

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:42.710
with only a couple of
minor interruptions,

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:46.490
Sweden had only
three prime ministers

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:50.630
between the early 1930s
and the late 1980s.

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:52.970
And I'll go over them briefly.

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:59.030
You had Per Albin Hansson,
who was the FDR of Sweden.

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:06.460
He came to power in 1932, ruled
till the end of World War II,

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:09.010
kept Sweden neutral
in the war, which

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.730
will be important
later on, and created

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:16.510
this folkhemmet ideology.

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:19.960
Then his successor
was Tage Erlander.

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:26.680
This was the great divisor
of big socialist programs,

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:29.890
kind of like Sweden's
equivalent of LBJ.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:33.370
But if you imagine LBJ ruling
for an entire generation,

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:37.690
you get an idea of what an
impact Tage Erlander had.

00:14:37.690 --> 00:14:40.380
And then, finally,
there was Olof Palme,

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:45.490
who first came to power in
1969 and ruled most of the time

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:51.310
until he was assassinated
mysteriously in 1986.

00:14:51.310 --> 00:14:54.200
He is a tendency we didn't
have in this country.

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:57.640
He's what maybe the United
States would have been like

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:00.820
if, say, Ted Kennedy,
instead of Ronald Reagan,

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:03.550
had been president in the 1980s.

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:08.020
So Hansson,
Erlander, and Palme--

00:15:08.020 --> 00:15:12.330
these are giants of
European socialism.

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:16.840
And over that period,
it is hard to think

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:21.700
of another triumvirate who
held such an important position

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:23.170
for so long.

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:26.260
And the only one I can
think of, actually,

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:29.920
is Ted Williams,
Carl Yastrzemski,

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:33.250
and Jim Rice in left field
for the Boston Red Sox.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:39.940
All three of them-- same period
of time, '30s to the '80s--

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:42.760
all three of them
in the Hall of Fame.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:45.920
We can talk about the Red Sox
instead, if anyone would like.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:50.710
But anyway, under those men,
anyhow, the Social Democratic

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.620
Party dominated Sweden.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.520
We're talking about from the
end of the Hoover administration

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.620
to the end of the
Reagan administration.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:02.560
We're talking about
from the Wall Street

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:07.340
crash in the Great Depression
until the internet age.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:13.810
And so think of these people
with their American analogs.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:21.850
If you had FDR, LBJ, and Ted
Kennedy given half a century,

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:25.180
unlimited resources,
and zero opposition

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:28.400
to build their own
kind of socialism,

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:31.280
what would it have looked like?

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:34.580
And I'll give you some examples
of what it did look like.

00:16:34.580 --> 00:16:37.900
Let's start with one
typical and really ambitious

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:40.510
program that would
be very consequential

00:16:40.510 --> 00:16:42.460
for the future of Sweden.

00:16:42.460 --> 00:16:47.590
In the early 1960s,
Erlander declared

00:16:47.590 --> 00:16:52.870
that all Swedes had a right
to modern humane housing

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:57.490
and that he would build 1
million units of housing.

00:16:57.490 --> 00:17:01.960
Now, given that Sweden had only
6 million people at the time,

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:04.750
that's a ton of housing.

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:06.490
It created a glut of building.

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:10.942
It drove up the construction
and workers' salaries.

00:17:10.942 --> 00:17:12.609
So it did some things
that people liked,

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:16.369
but it created problems, OK?

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:19.750
This was called the Million
Programme for the number

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:21.339
of units he was going to build.

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:25.210
Most of these Million
Programme housing units--

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:30.130
they were apartments
in big complexes

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:32.200
on the outskirts of town.

00:17:35.950 --> 00:17:37.960
Sweden has a relatively
small population.

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:40.930
It's got about the same
population as Michigan,

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:42.790
but it's twice as
big as Michigan,

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:45.370
so it's actually
got a lot of room.

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:47.170
Swedes don't have
to live that way.

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:51.340
They don't tend to like
living in apartments.

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:53.740
And the result was that a
lot of these apartments built

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:58.077
in the '60s were either
empty, or in very low demand

00:17:58.077 --> 00:17:58.660
in the future.

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.870
And that's going to be an
important consideration, which

00:18:01.870 --> 00:18:05.620
I'll come back to in
a couple of minutes.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:09.010
But there were impressive things
about-- or seemingly impressive

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:12.880
things about-- this Swedish
welfare state, things

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:17.800
where people seemed to be
able to have their cake

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:20.620
and eat it too.

00:18:20.620 --> 00:18:25.810
First of all, it seemed to offer
generous wages and equality

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:29.230
without denting
anybody's work ethic,

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:32.990
and generous welfare plans.

00:18:32.990 --> 00:18:38.440
Second, it had huge
top-income tax rates,

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.440
but it didn't seem to limit
entrepreneurship at all.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.793
And in fact, as I
said, Sweden was--

00:18:44.793 --> 00:18:46.210
for much of the
last century-- one

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:50.740
of the most entrepreneurial and
innovative countries on earth.

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:58.070
So in the old days, you had
Electrolux vacuum cleaners.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:18:59.740
You had Volvo.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:00.340
You had Saab.

00:19:00.340 --> 00:19:04.570
Saab was, originally,
an airplane company.

00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:07.750
Sweden, by the way, I believe,
is the only country of its size

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:09.940
that makes its own fighter jets.

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:12.820
Then you had retailers.

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:16.960
The Swedes
revolutionized retail.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:19.510
We didn't realize it until
the global economy started.

00:19:19.510 --> 00:19:23.680
But companies like IKEA, H&amp;M--

00:19:23.680 --> 00:19:25.480
those are all
companies in Sweden,

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:27.580
but they really made
quite an impression

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:28.880
in our part of the world.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:30.970
And then, on top
of that, you have

00:19:30.970 --> 00:19:37.750
the newer high-tech companies
like Spotify or Ericsson

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:38.540
phones.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:42.070
I mean, it's a very
impressive business culture.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:50.360
There's a young Kurdish Swedish
economist, Tino Sanandaji,

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.500
whose family
immigrated to Sweden

00:19:53.500 --> 00:19:57.340
at the turn of the
century to point out

00:19:57.340 --> 00:20:06.550
that this reputation for
being an entrepreneurial place

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:09.680
is not an illusion.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:13.060
The reputation for being able
to unite entrepreneurship

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:16.180
and socialism kind of
is, because if you really

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:18.940
look at it, if you look
at the 1950s as the time

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.760
when the Swedish welfare
state started operating

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.940
at full throttle, then
almost all of these companies

00:20:24.940 --> 00:20:27.790
were either already
there before it started

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:30.430
or they were founded
after it really

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:34.060
began to decline
which, I would say,

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:35.950
came at the start of the 1990s.

00:20:35.950 --> 00:20:40.660
And I'll talk a little
bit about how it declined.

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:44.500
The Swedish welfare state,
the Swedish socialist system,

00:20:44.500 --> 00:20:48.570
was surprisingly disciplined.

00:20:48.570 --> 00:20:51.540
And that's how they
succeeded in doing things

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:53.200
that no one thought
were possible

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:55.060
for a socialist system.

00:20:55.060 --> 00:21:00.640
But after the 1980s, Sweden
really began to do what

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:03.280
run-of-the-mill socialist
countries are all supposed

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:09.970
to do if you believe the models,
which is it let its benefits

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:16.260
run beyond the taxes that it
was raising to pay for them.

00:21:16.260 --> 00:21:18.070
And that's a
run-of-the-mill problem,

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:21.280
but there were problems
specific to Sweden, too.

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:23.830
They had a complicated
scheme of what

00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:28.300
they call wage-earner
investment funds, which

00:21:28.300 --> 00:21:31.480
was a tax that was
meant to be used

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:36.760
to slowly buy out private firms
and turn them into public ones.

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:42.860
And that wound up sowing
a lot of insecurity.

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:45.370
I think the country
was also a bit

00:21:45.370 --> 00:21:49.030
caught by surprise
by the competition

00:21:49.030 --> 00:21:51.250
that the end of the
Cold War had unleashed.

00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:54.550
So I think it lost a lot
of its shipbuilding, which

00:21:54.550 --> 00:21:59.590
was a major industry, to
foreign countries like Korea.

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:04.960
But basically,
between 1990 and 1994,

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:09.730
Sweden's GNP contracted by 6%
and its level of employment

00:22:09.730 --> 00:22:11.080
fell by 12%.

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:15.730
It's one of the largest
peacetime contractions

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:18.280
that we know of in
any single country.

00:22:18.280 --> 00:22:22.720
There are global downturns,
but this is just one country

00:22:22.720 --> 00:22:24.370
falling through
the floor at a time

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:27.430
of general global prosperity.

00:22:27.430 --> 00:22:30.160
And by that time,
Sweden was dealing

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.550
with another big problem,
which is immigration.

00:22:33.550 --> 00:22:37.000
Now I'll talk about
that for a little bit.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:42.610
When I was writing a
book about immigration

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:46.810
in Europe several years
ago, ever since then, I've

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:49.090
been asked, what
country in Europe

00:22:49.090 --> 00:22:54.310
is really in the worst shape
faced with immigration?

00:22:54.310 --> 00:23:00.760
And I never have any hesitation
in answering, Sweden.

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:04.060
Sweden's immigration problems
are maybe not quite as obvious

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.430
as the ones in, say, France.

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:09.250
And they take a
little bit of tracing.

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:13.020
Their origins go back to the
middle of the last century.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.160
But I'd like to
explain why I think

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:18.730
Sweden has such a big problem.

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:22.450
Sweden was officially
neutral in World War

00:23:22.450 --> 00:23:26.530
II, which means that
its prosperity was not

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:29.660
interrupted by the war.

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:34.330
But in fact, it was also a
big supplier of iron ore,

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:37.540
in particular-- and the
machinery, to a lesser degree--

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:41.200
to the Nazi government.

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:48.050
So when 1945 came, Sweden
was really well capitalized.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:51.550
And this was a time when all
of Europe needed to be rebuilt.

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:54.880
And Sweden was the only
advanced Western country

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:59.110
besides the United States that
had its entire industrial base

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:00.110
intact.

00:24:00.110 --> 00:24:03.430
And so Sweden was going
to get rich, rich, rich.

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:07.430
I mean, in retrospect, Sweden's
post-war growth rates--

00:24:07.430 --> 00:24:10.420
there was something
inevitable about them.

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:16.990
Sweden grew 4% a year between
the end of the Second World War

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:21.500
and the oil crisis of 1973.

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:23.530
And it's from that,
I think, that a lot

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:27.970
of this idea of the Swedish
welfare state as a real miracle

00:24:27.970 --> 00:24:28.510
came about.

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:33.410
In fact, Sweden grew
7% a year in the 1960s,

00:24:33.410 --> 00:24:38.050
which was of a Chinese rate.

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:43.090
But the economy was so hot
that Sweden needed labor.

00:24:43.090 --> 00:24:48.340
And it just needed more hands
to keep these factories running.

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:52.030
And so the result was a
series of labor agreements

00:24:52.030 --> 00:24:57.100
with foreign countries along
the lines of the gastarbeiter

00:24:57.100 --> 00:25:01.840
programs-- the guest worker
programs-- that Germany had.

00:25:01.840 --> 00:25:04.780
So Sweden started by
offering special deals

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:08.285
to Italy and Hungary in 1947.

00:25:08.285 --> 00:25:09.910
And then, in the
'60s, when they worked

00:25:09.910 --> 00:25:12.580
through those
workforces, they wound up

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:15.190
offering the same to
Yugoslavia and Turkey.

00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:19.970
Finns-- many Finns
are Swedish-speaking.

00:25:19.970 --> 00:25:22.360
They were coming in
throughout the same period.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:27.760
And this immigration
was quite a success.

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:34.030
And the problem was that
they couldn't shut it off,

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:36.700
even when the economy
began to go south.

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:44.820
This is because of certain
moral considerations

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:46.860
in foreign policy.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:50.940
Sweden had always had a--

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:52.620
you could call it
a pacifist country.

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:56.040
They didn't participate in
World War I or World War II.

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:59.160
But just because you don't
want to get wrecked in war,

00:25:59.160 --> 00:26:02.820
it doesn't mean you necessarily
renounce your aspiration

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:04.830
to shape the world.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:10.470
So Sweden has always had a
crusading, humanitarian side.

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:14.370
And perhaps it's a way of
atoning for its cooperation

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:17.280
with Nazi Germany
in World War II.

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:19.410
It became much
more demonstrative

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:23.730
about this side of itself.

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:29.810
In 1968, there was some
persecution of Jews in Poland.

00:26:29.810 --> 00:26:30.900
And many left.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:32.400
Sweden took them.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:38.790
Sweden took all the Greeks,
offered spots to all the Greeks

00:26:38.790 --> 00:26:41.790
who felt themselves
persecuted by the government

00:26:41.790 --> 00:26:44.910
of the colonels
between 1968 and--

00:26:44.910 --> 00:26:47.340
I believe it was-- '74.

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:50.010
It took a lot of
people in from Chile.

00:26:50.010 --> 00:26:53.790
It was a main
destination for leftists

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:56.400
who felt they had to
leave Chile after the coup

00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:02.190
by General Pinochet
in, I believe, '73.

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:11.020
But then, after Olof
Palme came to power--

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:14.630
he's the third of the
leaders that I mentioned.

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.900
He was a much more
anti-American, progressive

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:19.340
internationalist.

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:20.780
And when he came
to power, I think

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:24.710
Sweden lost the habit of
saying, no, altogether.

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:27.470
It was then that
Swedish politicians

00:27:27.470 --> 00:27:30.830
started referring to
their country as a, quote,

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:33.800
"moral superpower," unquote.

00:27:33.800 --> 00:27:38.120
So Sweden took refugees
from the Somali War,

00:27:38.120 --> 00:27:42.380
from the Bosnian War
in the early '90s.

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:45.380
They took refugees from Algeria.

00:27:45.380 --> 00:27:50.420
They took Iraqis and
Afghans in the new century.

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:54.770
And then, four
years ago, in 2015--

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:58.700
if you remember when refugees
from the war in Syria

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:02.660
began marching into Europe--

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:06.440
the Scandinavian countries
were, most people believed,

00:28:06.440 --> 00:28:08.750
extraordinarily generous.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:11.810
And I'll give you the number
of refugees they took.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:17.990
Denmark processed 21,000
asylum applications.

00:28:17.990 --> 00:28:21.290
Norway processed 31,000.

00:28:21.290 --> 00:28:23.790
Finland processed 32,000.

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:29.310
And Sweden took 162,000.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:33.860
And this has turned the
country of Sweden upside down.

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:38.190
Many European countries have
tried to limit immigration

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:40.190
over the years on the
grounds that they wouldn't

00:28:40.190 --> 00:28:42.980
know where to put them.

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:46.440
This is not a problem that
Sweden had, at least at first,

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:50.300
because it had all this housing
that it had built in places

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:53.070
outside of major cities.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:58.130
So in housing projects in
places like Rinkaby and Tensta,

00:28:58.130 --> 00:29:00.650
outside of Stockholm,
these places

00:29:00.650 --> 00:29:03.980
tended to fill up
with foreigners.

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:07.640
Now, we should not
exaggerate the role

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.290
of the Million Programme.

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:12.590
I mean, immigration,
by now, has outstripped

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:17.160
the ability even of Sweden
to provide crisis housing.

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:20.900
And since 2015, it
seems that every summer

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:26.510
camp, and off-season
hotel, and gymnasium floor

00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:30.860
has been used to
house immigrant beds.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:37.190
But until recently, there were
places to hide immigrants.

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:40.220
And this is a very
important thing

00:29:40.220 --> 00:29:42.230
if you want to
understand why Sweden

00:29:42.230 --> 00:29:44.120
has taken so many migrants.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:46.850
In Stockholm, you don't
notice that you're

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:51.020
living in a highly-diverse
metropolitan area

00:29:51.020 --> 00:29:55.550
in the single European country
that has been most overwhelmed

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:57.570
by migration.

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:00.710
In fact, it looks like
a low-migration country.

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:03.470
But in fact, it's not a
low-migration country.

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:05.540
It's a segregated country.

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:12.780
And what you have is the
usual paradox of segregation--

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:15.490
which is, the more
segregated a country

00:30:15.490 --> 00:30:18.920
is, the bigger a problem it
has, but the more harmonious

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:21.320
it looks.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:24.740
And so that's where
you are with Sweden.

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:27.350
All over Europe
now, there's talk

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:30.710
about how, if migration
isn't slowed down,

00:30:30.710 --> 00:30:34.610
this or that country is going
to lose its culture, et cetera.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:37.520
In Sweden, it's already
too late for that.

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:41.030
And the numbers in Sweden
are really extraordinary.

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:44.990
Sweden's population
is 19% foreign-born.

00:30:44.990 --> 00:30:48.230
It is 32% of foreign origin.

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:52.820
More than 30% of the babies
are born to foreign mothers.

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:58.070
Right now, Sweden's
Muslim population is 8%.

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:01.700
According to a Pew
Research Center,

00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:05.360
if immigration continues
at the present rate,

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:07.440
in the year 2050--

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:09.440
which is 31 years from now--

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:13.280
Sweden will be 30% Muslim.

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:17.750
If refugee flows
were to stop today,

00:31:17.750 --> 00:31:19.970
and there would be zero
migration for the next 30

00:31:19.970 --> 00:31:22.670
years, Pew projects
that Sweden would still

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:27.480
be 21% Muslim in the year 2050.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:33.320
And this is important when we
think about Swedish socialism,

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:39.530
because the peculiarities
that made Sweden special are

00:31:39.530 --> 00:31:40.790
disappearing.

00:31:40.790 --> 00:31:43.730
And these, I think,
are the peculiarities

00:31:43.730 --> 00:31:48.920
that made it possible to sustain
socialism for so long there.

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:54.290
So really radical parties have
begun to crop up in Sweden.

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:57.410
They've begun to get
double-digit results

00:31:57.410 --> 00:31:59.270
in elections.

00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:02.480
And what they generally
complain about is,

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:05.990
primarily, the size of
immigration, but also

00:32:05.990 --> 00:32:08.810
the effect of the size
of immigration on what

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:12.200
remains of the
socialist welfare state.

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:17.480
So Tino Sanandaji, the economist
whose insightful remarks

00:32:17.480 --> 00:32:22.040
on entrepreneurship I
mentioned just a second ago,

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:26.540
he pointed out that the
population of Swedes

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:32.330
of Swedish origin is roughly
stable at 7.7 million

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:36.500
but that the country as a
whole, due to immigration,

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:38.930
now has over 10 million people.

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:43.370
And it's growing at a rate
as fast as Bangladesh.

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:46.070
Sanandaji is a very
interesting guy.

00:32:46.070 --> 00:32:48.260
He's a very
mischievous economist.

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:50.100
And he loves to throw
out these factoids.

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:51.710
It's just his way.

00:32:51.710 --> 00:32:53.810
One other thing
he's done is he's

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:58.860
tallied the net benefit
per migrant of migration.

00:32:58.860 --> 00:33:03.120
And he's found that
it's a net loss.

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:07.940
I should just say, he's
a very fascinating guy.

00:33:07.940 --> 00:33:12.620
He's much criticized
on talk shows

00:33:12.620 --> 00:33:14.780
and by proper intellectuals.

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:20.570
But he self published a book
on this subject of migration,

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:23.060
from which I draw some
of these statistics.

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:27.830
And it became the number-one
bestseller in Sweden,

00:33:27.830 --> 00:33:30.420
for quite a while.

00:33:30.420 --> 00:33:33.780
But anyway, I would say
that maybe, in fact,

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:37.730
the greatest gift of
immigration to Sweden

00:33:37.730 --> 00:33:42.770
has been a set of
immigrant intellectuals

00:33:42.770 --> 00:33:47.630
who have revealed the
Swedes to themselves,

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:49.610
or revealed things
about their new country

00:33:49.610 --> 00:33:52.880
that the Swedes themselves
had a hard time noticing.

00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:55.820
So I've already
mentioned Tino Sanandaji,

00:33:55.820 --> 00:34:01.130
but I would like to mention
in particular the economist

00:34:01.130 --> 00:34:04.250
and politician Mauricio
Rojas, because I

00:34:04.250 --> 00:34:06.690
find his models so interesting.

00:34:06.690 --> 00:34:10.310
They taught me so much about
Sweden when I was studying it.

00:34:10.310 --> 00:34:13.580
I met him about
15 years ago when

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:18.590
I was writing a lot about
immigration and Sweden's

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:19.580
growing inner cities.

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:21.800
And Rojas was a figure.

00:34:21.800 --> 00:34:26.540
He was the closest thing in
Sweden to, say, Jack Kemp.

00:34:26.540 --> 00:34:28.670
He was the guy who
was testing out

00:34:28.670 --> 00:34:34.969
the appeal of free-market ideas
to non-traditional voters.

00:34:34.969 --> 00:34:39.810
And Rojas arrived in Sweden--

00:34:39.810 --> 00:34:42.590
as you may guess,
from his name--

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:47.630
from Chile as a young
leftist in the 1970s.

00:34:47.630 --> 00:34:52.310
And he was a refugee
from a country

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:56.750
that had put all its effort
into building socialism.

00:34:56.750 --> 00:35:03.020
And the socialist project there
had met tremendous resistance,

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:07.220
not just from the middle class,
but eventually from the army.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:11.030
And so he suddenly
wound up in this country

00:35:11.030 --> 00:35:14.450
where 100% of the
people seemed to think

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:20.420
that socialism was a great
deal and that everyone

00:35:20.420 --> 00:35:21.890
should imitate it.

00:35:21.890 --> 00:35:26.240
And so he began to wonder, why?

00:35:26.240 --> 00:35:30.830
Why wasn't Chile
able to imitate it?

00:35:30.830 --> 00:35:32.790
Why couldn't
everyone imitate it?

00:35:32.790 --> 00:35:36.140
And the usual response
of people in Sweden

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:39.080
was, well, you didn't get
the program exactly right

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:40.850
in every detail.

00:35:40.850 --> 00:35:43.170
You have to
renominate the workers

00:35:43.170 --> 00:35:46.460
to the workers' councils
biannually instead of annually,

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:47.360
stuff like that.

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:51.860
And you know how a lot
of utopias are this way.

00:35:51.860 --> 00:35:53.390
They have a lot of promise.

00:35:53.390 --> 00:35:55.700
But you get one
tiny detail wrong,

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:58.370
and you wind up in a civil war.

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:00.587
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:00.587 --> 00:36:02.420
So when people were
talking to him that way,

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:08.300
Rojas said, no, it has to be
something deeper than that.

00:36:08.300 --> 00:36:11.180
That can't be the way
that Latin America

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:15.750
is going to get Swedish-style
socialism because, in fact,

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:20.400
that's not the way Sweden
got Swedish-style socialism.

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:24.140
And in his work as an
economic historian--

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:29.660
and a bit less so, but in
his career as a free-market

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:30.860
politician--

00:36:30.860 --> 00:36:38.270
he went slowly through Sweden's
long history and its culture,

00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:41.730
some of the things I've been
discussing this afternoon.

00:36:41.730 --> 00:36:45.710
And that, I think, was really
the right place to look.

00:36:45.710 --> 00:36:48.380
The most important
thing he discovered

00:36:48.380 --> 00:36:52.400
was how widely Sweden
diverged from the history

00:36:52.400 --> 00:36:54.540
of other countries.

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:55.760
It was a particular place.

00:36:55.760 --> 00:37:00.050
A lot of its particularities
came from its peasant origins.

00:37:00.050 --> 00:37:03.950
It had this tremendous
tradition of egalitarian

00:37:03.950 --> 00:37:07.220
that we've been talking about.

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:11.540
But it didn't have much of a
tradition of individual rights.

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:13.700
That was a strange
thing about it.

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:15.200
Then there were
other peculiarities

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:18.140
that came from its homogeneity.

00:37:18.140 --> 00:37:22.580
For instance, there was a
sense of shame in not working,

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:26.670
in not carrying your
share of the load.

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:32.030
And this homogeneity, Rojas
also found, extended to opinion.

00:37:32.030 --> 00:37:36.590
Swedes expected to
agree with one another.

00:37:36.590 --> 00:37:40.760
And that made its
development very different

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:42.920
from other Western
European nations.

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:46.520
A lot of European
nations developed

00:37:46.520 --> 00:37:51.380
as a way of finding a compromise
between clashing interests--

00:37:51.380 --> 00:37:55.590
classes, or noble
houses, or even nations.

00:37:55.590 --> 00:38:00.720
I mean, the United Kingdom is
the classic example of that.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.390
But actually,
Sweden didn't really

00:38:02.390 --> 00:38:04.730
have many competing interests.

00:38:04.730 --> 00:38:07.760
And as such, there
were lots of things

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:09.980
that were typical of the
West that it didn't really

00:38:09.980 --> 00:38:18.660
have in quite the same way,
like freelance intellectuals,

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:20.940
an obsession with a free press.

00:38:20.940 --> 00:38:23.790
In Sweden, both
radio and television

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:26.700
were state monopolies
into the 1980s.

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:32.880
It didn't have a Western idea
of the separation of powers,

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:35.070
because the goal
was not arguing.

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:36.990
The goal was agreeing.

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:39.610
And so Rojas looked
at these things.

00:38:39.610 --> 00:38:41.610
And he said, well,
of course, you

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:44.220
can build socialism
in a place like that.

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:46.470
But most places
aren't like that.

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:49.590
And he asked people
to try to understand

00:38:49.590 --> 00:38:54.060
how strange Swedish
socialism was

00:38:54.060 --> 00:38:58.230
by asking them to look at more
normal, less controversial

00:38:58.230 --> 00:38:58.980
Swedish things.

00:38:58.980 --> 00:39:01.470
And he took the
example of a piece

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:06.810
of Swedish modern
furniture, like a table.

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:12.240
And I'll close with the
conclusion he drew from it.

00:39:12.240 --> 00:39:14.250
He said, the Swedes
look at a table.

00:39:14.250 --> 00:39:15.930
And they're so
provincial that they

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:17.480
think it's just a normal table.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:20.130
But Rojas, having
grown up in Chile,

00:39:20.130 --> 00:39:24.000
could see actually how
different the Swedish table was.

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:27.360
And I will quote
from an essay of his.

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:31.800
He says, "The fascinating
austerity and naturalism

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:35.010
of Nordic design,
the Swedish passion

00:39:35.010 --> 00:39:39.600
for simplicity and the
natural, the evident dislike

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:43.140
of purely ornamental or
ostentatious elements,

00:39:43.140 --> 00:39:47.850
the illegitimacy of luxury,
and a very sober relation

00:39:47.850 --> 00:39:49.050
to money--

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:52.170
all these are expressions
of a culture bearing

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:56.160
the mark of a still
unchallenged peasant heritage

00:39:56.160 --> 00:40:00.690
in which is deemed detestable
not only aristocratic pomp

00:40:00.690 --> 00:40:02.640
or individualistic
petty Bourgeois

00:40:02.640 --> 00:40:06.630
swagger, but also any attempt
to be noticeably different

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:09.390
from the masses of
the people or to act

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:12.790
without some kind of
collective cover."

00:40:12.790 --> 00:40:19.050
And I think, with that, Rojas
had discovered the solution

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:23.340
to the problem that we've spent
the last half hour discussing.

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:27.780
Swedish socialism arose
from peculiarities

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:30.540
of Sweden's national character.

00:40:30.540 --> 00:40:35.640
Not only that-- it relied on
social and economic capital

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:39.620
that had been built up before
Sweden became socialist.

00:40:39.620 --> 00:40:44.640
And this was not a program that
could be replicated elsewhere,

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:47.110
no matter how long
one studied it.

00:40:47.110 --> 00:40:50.400
In fact, it was not
a program at all.

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:53.310
It was a culture.

00:40:53.310 --> 00:40:54.840
If you have that
kind of culture,

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:59.210
it will probably be pretty
easy to build socialism.

00:40:59.210 --> 00:41:03.810
But if you don't, you'd
probably be wiser not to try.

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:04.690
Thank you very much.

00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:07.552
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:14.240 --> 00:41:15.650
SPEAKER: Thank
you, Mr. Caldwell.

00:41:15.650 --> 00:41:18.772
We now have time for Q and A,
so please come to the microphone

00:41:18.772 --> 00:41:19.730
if you have a question.

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:30.950
AUDIENCE: Hello, Mr. Caldwell.

00:41:30.950 --> 00:41:33.170
I think language is
important, especially

00:41:33.170 --> 00:41:37.250
if we're going to convince
people today who want socialism

00:41:37.250 --> 00:41:39.500
here that we shouldn't have it.

00:41:39.500 --> 00:41:45.080
And you make quite a case for a
collectivist mindset in Sweden,

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:51.650
along with their homogeneous
society, until recently.

00:41:51.650 --> 00:41:53.900
But many of the
other speakers here

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:57.380
would have said that Sweden
really doesn't have socialism

00:41:57.380 --> 00:42:00.020
because the government
doesn't own the agents

00:42:00.020 --> 00:42:02.760
or means of production.

00:42:02.760 --> 00:42:06.680
So how would you reconcile
your calling what Sweden has

00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:10.095
socialism, as opposed to those
who would say they really

00:42:10.095 --> 00:42:10.595
don't?

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:15.800
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
That's a good question.

00:42:15.800 --> 00:42:18.960
One is, it's very egalitarian.

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:24.980
It's highly redistributive.

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:28.490
The other is there is a
government role in many

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:32.160
of Sweden's largest industries.

00:42:32.160 --> 00:42:38.170
And I think the goal of,
certainly, the Erlander

00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:39.430
government--

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:40.450
I believe, as they put--

00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:47.020
was to decommodify social goods.

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:50.440
So you're right, and
that's the reason

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:52.300
I think that we're
talking about it today.

00:42:52.300 --> 00:43:02.350
It was not a full-blown
Soviet-style experiment

00:43:02.350 --> 00:43:06.640
in rejiggering a society.

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:08.110
But it went very far.

00:43:08.110 --> 00:43:12.280
It went farther in the
direction of redistribution

00:43:12.280 --> 00:43:15.660
than probably any
other European society.

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:18.310
And that was what I think
people meant by that word.

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:27.710
AUDIENCE: Yes, thank you very
much for a very interesting

00:43:27.710 --> 00:43:30.050
perspective on Sweden.

00:43:30.050 --> 00:43:38.180
We had an exchange student
from Sweden in 1974, Annika.

00:43:38.180 --> 00:43:41.000
And she was so Swedish--

00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:45.500
blonde hair, beautiful eyes.

00:43:45.500 --> 00:43:51.050
When she walked down the
streets of our tiny little town

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:57.470
in northern Michigan, people
would just stop and stare.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:03.140
The people in
Rogers City thought

00:44:03.140 --> 00:44:08.840
that they knew a lot about
Sweden, because they had--

00:44:08.840 --> 00:44:12.580
[CLEARS THROAT]
excuse me-- saunas.

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:15.520
[LAUGHTER]

00:44:19.940 --> 00:44:28.410
Anyway, my feeling about
the politicians today

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:33.600
who are pushing
socialism is they

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:36.996
don't want us to be Americans.

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:44.280
Americans are unique.

00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:50.000
Americans like being wealthy.

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:52.160
We want to be wealthy.

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:54.860
That's why we work so hard.

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:58.110
And what do we do
with our wealth?

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:03.900
We try to make
other people happy.

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:05.580
We work hard.

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:10.730
And when I was young, my mother
would say, you're not French,

00:45:10.730 --> 00:45:12.590
you're not Dutch.

00:45:12.590 --> 00:45:16.150
You're an American.

00:45:16.150 --> 00:45:18.250
What do you think about that?

00:45:18.250 --> 00:45:21.244
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:28.240 --> 00:45:30.370
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
Well, I think

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:33.940
that I was making a
pretty similar point--

00:45:33.940 --> 00:45:39.740
that the different countries
have different cultures.

00:45:39.740 --> 00:45:49.000
When you live in a vast and
varied culture like our own,

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:51.460
where a lot of people actually
don't know each other,

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:58.510
and there is not a lot of
communal provision of security,

00:45:58.510 --> 00:46:06.250
you will need actually to
aspire to accumulate money.

00:46:06.250 --> 00:46:10.780
I think that that need was
less pressing for people

00:46:10.780 --> 00:46:13.930
who came from a Swedish
culture, or at least it

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:16.780
was perceived as less pressing.

00:46:16.780 --> 00:46:20.560
I think that it's
become more pressing

00:46:20.560 --> 00:46:25.720
as their society has globalized,
as there are lots of new things

00:46:25.720 --> 00:46:30.430
that leave them uneasy.

00:46:30.430 --> 00:46:35.180
And I think that's one of the
reasons why Sweden is changing,

00:46:35.180 --> 00:46:37.780
one of the reasons why
the Swedes themselves

00:46:37.780 --> 00:46:40.210
are growing more skeptical
about their welfare state.

00:46:43.247 --> 00:46:45.080
AUDIENCE: Thank you so
much for coming here.

00:46:45.080 --> 00:46:48.230
I, myself, have never hosted
a Swedish exchange student.

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:50.640
But I do have a
question for you.

00:46:50.640 --> 00:46:53.330
So clearly, mass
immigration into Sweden

00:46:53.330 --> 00:46:56.755
negatively affected Swedish
social and public institutions.

00:46:56.755 --> 00:46:58.130
And my question
for you would be,

00:46:58.130 --> 00:47:00.290
what effects do you
believe mass immigration

00:47:00.290 --> 00:47:03.740
into our own country will have
on our institutions in society?

00:47:03.740 --> 00:47:05.235
And if those effects
are negative,

00:47:05.235 --> 00:47:06.860
how does the threat
of mass immigration

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:09.890
really square up to a
few socialist politicians

00:47:09.890 --> 00:47:12.056
running for national office?

00:47:12.056 --> 00:47:12.556
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:12.556 --> 00:47:14.014
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
Uh-huh, yeah.

00:47:14.014 --> 00:47:15.700
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:16.340 --> 00:47:18.350
Immigration has been--

00:47:18.350 --> 00:47:23.570
I mean, I think immigration is
disruptive wherever it happens.

00:47:23.570 --> 00:47:26.820
It has benefits to
wherever it happens.

00:47:26.820 --> 00:47:29.060
But the balance is
different than Europe

00:47:29.060 --> 00:47:34.580
because, in most
European countries--

00:47:34.580 --> 00:47:38.810
and I'm sure this
is true in Sweden--

00:47:38.810 --> 00:47:41.780
it's not considered OK to have
people come into your country

00:47:41.780 --> 00:47:44.180
who cannot have
access to benefits.

00:47:44.180 --> 00:47:48.680
As soon as immigrants
make themselves known,

00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:50.840
they are eligible for
a lot of benefits.

00:47:50.840 --> 00:47:58.940
And there's been a big outcry,
and it's probably more limited

00:47:58.940 --> 00:48:02.450
than it has been in
recent waves of migration.

00:48:02.450 --> 00:48:06.350
But if you're a Swedish migrant,
if you're a migrant coming

00:48:06.350 --> 00:48:09.230
into Sweden, you get a check.

00:48:09.230 --> 00:48:13.520
Now, that means that they can
exist on the fringes of society

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:14.020
forever.

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:20.520
There is not the need to
assimilate the way there was--

00:48:20.520 --> 00:48:25.820
I mean, the American system
of tolerating illegal

00:48:25.820 --> 00:48:28.145
immigration-- where people
are living at the margins--

00:48:32.570 --> 00:48:34.570
it has its
imperfections, obviously.

00:48:34.570 --> 00:48:37.290
For one thing, it creates
illegality, et cetera.

00:48:37.290 --> 00:48:42.000
But it does force people
into the workplace.

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:44.240
And I think the workplace
is the main place

00:48:44.240 --> 00:48:46.970
that people get assimilated.

00:48:46.970 --> 00:48:53.400
And that does not happen to
the same degree in Sweden.

00:48:53.400 --> 00:49:01.070
So I think that's the main way
in which Sweden is being more

00:49:01.070 --> 00:49:03.300
negatively impacted than here.

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:07.580
There's another issue
that comes up in Europe.

00:49:07.580 --> 00:49:11.150
And that is the fact your
immigrants, generally--

00:49:11.150 --> 00:49:15.630
it's changed a bit in the
last 10 years, or 15 years.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:20.090
But the great pool
of immigrants in most

00:49:20.090 --> 00:49:24.000
of these Western societies
come from adjacent cultures.

00:49:24.000 --> 00:49:28.910
So the tone of
American immigration

00:49:28.910 --> 00:49:31.610
is set by Latin Americans.

00:49:31.610 --> 00:49:34.040
The tone of European
immigration is

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:38.840
set by North African and
Middle Eastern Muslims.

00:49:38.840 --> 00:49:40.350
This is a subjective thing.

00:49:40.350 --> 00:49:43.820
But I would say that the gap
between American and Latin

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:47.960
American culture is
extremely narrow, compared

00:49:47.960 --> 00:49:52.190
to the gap between European
and Muslim culture.

00:49:52.190 --> 00:49:53.270
That's a problem.

00:49:53.270 --> 00:49:56.030
It's a bit less of
a problem in Sweden,

00:49:56.030 --> 00:50:00.920
because you have such a big
diversity of Muslim cultures.

00:50:00.920 --> 00:50:02.810
So it's not like you have--

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:04.580
as you do in Germany, say--

00:50:04.580 --> 00:50:07.700
a huge Turkish
subculture that's like

00:50:07.700 --> 00:50:11.720
a country within a country,
or Algerians in France,

00:50:11.720 --> 00:50:15.740
or Moroccans in the Netherlands.

00:50:15.740 --> 00:50:21.080
But it does create
expectations of the country

00:50:21.080 --> 00:50:22.430
and of its institutions.

00:50:22.430 --> 00:50:23.930
And there's not
time to go into it,

00:50:23.930 --> 00:50:30.350
but you can consider the
debates over halal meat

00:50:30.350 --> 00:50:35.330
in public schools where
probably a third of the kids

00:50:35.330 --> 00:50:37.280
are foreign born.

00:50:37.280 --> 00:50:42.800
And you have a measure of
how migration actually puts

00:50:42.800 --> 00:50:47.674
a bigger stress on Sweden,
I think, than it does on us.

00:50:47.674 --> 00:50:50.170
AUDIENCE: [CLEARS THROAT] Sorry.

00:50:50.170 --> 00:50:53.570
So echoing the first question,
evading the question of

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:56.210
whether or not something is
or is not doctoral socialism,

00:50:56.210 --> 00:50:58.160
I know that Norway
and Sweden tend

00:50:58.160 --> 00:51:00.740
to have a Canada-America-type
comparison when we're talking

00:51:00.740 --> 00:51:03.053
about Scandinavian economies.

00:51:03.053 --> 00:51:04.970
I know that Norwegians
really pride themselves

00:51:04.970 --> 00:51:07.530
on having handled an
immigration crisis a lot better

00:51:07.530 --> 00:51:09.740
and that they have,
famously, oil money that

00:51:09.740 --> 00:51:10.940
keeps them floating.

00:51:10.940 --> 00:51:13.023
So I'm wondering if you
could expound a little bit

00:51:13.023 --> 00:51:16.880
on contrasting Norwegian
social democratic concepts

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:18.840
and Swedish social
democratic concepts,

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:21.370
especially as Sweden
has become more,

00:51:21.370 --> 00:51:23.120
I should say, socially
democratic and less

00:51:23.120 --> 00:51:25.050
straight-up socialist.

00:51:25.050 --> 00:51:29.060
So how do those
two come together?

00:51:29.060 --> 00:51:30.920
Sweden's, obviously,
a dumpster fire.

00:51:30.920 --> 00:51:33.250
And Norway prides itself on
not being a dumpster fire.

00:51:33.250 --> 00:51:35.250
And it seems to be the
only difference is that--

00:51:35.250 --> 00:51:36.470
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
Not being a what?

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:37.790
AUDIENCE: A dumpster
fire, right?

00:51:37.790 --> 00:51:38.510
Like [EXPLOSION EFFECTS].

00:51:38.510 --> 00:51:39.090
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
Oh, oh, oh, oh,

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:40.798
yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:51:40.798 --> 00:51:41.870
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:41.870 --> 00:51:47.030
AUDIENCE: So other than handling
immigration well and having

00:51:47.030 --> 00:51:51.138
a ton of reserve oil, is there
something magical about Norway?

00:51:51.138 --> 00:51:53.180
I mean, they both have
the Protestant work ethic,

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:55.790
as Sanandaji would say.

00:51:55.790 --> 00:51:57.960
Yeah, could you just expound
on that a little bit?

00:51:57.960 --> 00:51:59.675
Because I find Norway to
be an interesting contrast

00:51:59.675 --> 00:52:00.260
with Sweden.

00:52:00.260 --> 00:52:01.010
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
Yeah, I'm not

00:52:01.010 --> 00:52:03.470
sure I can expand on that,
because that would require

00:52:03.470 --> 00:52:08.780
a greater knowledge of the
Norwegian immigrant system

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:11.070
than I actually have.

00:52:11.070 --> 00:52:16.490
A contrast that I have noted,
that I wrote quite a lot

00:52:16.490 --> 00:52:20.300
about in past years, was
between Denmark and Sweden,

00:52:20.300 --> 00:52:26.690
because it's very obvious
where Malmo in Sweden

00:52:26.690 --> 00:52:29.830
meets Copenhagen and Denmark.

00:52:29.830 --> 00:52:33.480
There was a bridge built
there a couple of decades ago.

00:52:33.480 --> 00:52:36.000
And so they've become
kind of like twin cities.

00:52:36.000 --> 00:52:40.430
And Denmark has-- since 2001--

00:52:40.430 --> 00:52:47.750
had a really draconian
anti-immigrant regime.

00:52:47.750 --> 00:52:51.960
Let's say, it's not an immigrant
ban, or anything like that.

00:52:51.960 --> 00:52:55.340
But there are a series of
laws meant to prevent things

00:52:55.340 --> 00:52:58.070
like chain migration--

00:52:58.070 --> 00:53:03.050
that is, groups of
migrants importing brides

00:53:03.050 --> 00:53:06.620
from their villages, who then
have children, who bring brides

00:53:06.620 --> 00:53:09.900
from their villages, and the
children being brought up

00:53:09.900 --> 00:53:10.900
in the foreign language.

00:53:10.900 --> 00:53:14.030
This happens a lot with
people from Pakistan

00:53:14.030 --> 00:53:16.460
and from eastern Turkey.

00:53:20.280 --> 00:53:22.530
The Danish attempt
to limit these things

00:53:22.530 --> 00:53:28.500
has resulted in a drastically
different social profile,

00:53:28.500 --> 00:53:29.520
I think.

00:53:29.520 --> 00:53:35.170
I think Denmark's foreign born
percentages is well under 10%.

00:53:35.170 --> 00:53:37.580
And Sweden's is at 20%.

00:53:37.580 --> 00:53:39.150
I don't think
Denmark is considered

00:53:39.150 --> 00:53:45.480
a really intolerant country.

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:47.940
But they have a very
different relationship

00:53:47.940 --> 00:53:53.670
to migration now
than Sweden does.

00:53:53.670 --> 00:54:00.565
And so I think that's the
comparison I've looked at.

00:54:00.565 --> 00:54:02.440
I'm sorry I can't tell
you more about Norway.

00:54:05.690 --> 00:54:09.520
AUDIENCE: I have a question
regarding the Swedish elections

00:54:09.520 --> 00:54:14.088
of 1928 and 1932
and the distinction

00:54:14.088 --> 00:54:15.880
you see between the
perception of socialism

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:19.780
in 1928 and in 1932 and
the rise of the folkhemmet.

00:54:19.780 --> 00:54:21.640
Can that be attributed
to, or is it

00:54:21.640 --> 00:54:25.240
partially attributable
to, the ripple effects

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:28.980
of the international recession
that goes on in the 1930s--

00:54:28.980 --> 00:54:33.970
the Great Depression
in the United States,

00:54:33.970 --> 00:54:38.260
and the financial insolvency
of Germany, and France,

00:54:38.260 --> 00:54:39.783
and Britain-- to some extent?

00:54:39.783 --> 00:54:41.200
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL:
Yes, I think

00:54:41.200 --> 00:54:42.450
it's a very troubled time.

00:54:42.450 --> 00:54:43.450
And there are a lot of--

00:54:47.890 --> 00:54:49.930
the closer one looks
at Sweden, the more one

00:54:49.930 --> 00:54:52.740
sees peculiarities.

00:54:52.740 --> 00:54:54.840
It's tempting to
look at all these

00:54:54.840 --> 00:54:57.640
as, here are things that
were happening in Europe.

00:54:57.640 --> 00:55:00.420
But the countries
were quite different.

00:55:00.420 --> 00:55:02.340
And Sweden, when
you think of it,

00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:06.130
is really across
a rather large sea

00:55:06.130 --> 00:55:08.540
from the mainland of Europe.

00:55:08.540 --> 00:55:11.530
And so one thing
that happened, when

00:55:11.530 --> 00:55:16.390
you talk about the transition
from just a purely socialist

00:55:16.390 --> 00:55:20.650
program to a socialist
nationalist program,

00:55:20.650 --> 00:55:25.120
is that Sweden didn't
have a real right

00:55:25.120 --> 00:55:29.440
wing that had claimed
that nationalist mantle.

00:55:29.440 --> 00:55:38.560
And I think it's
because, in World War I,

00:55:38.560 --> 00:55:40.240
Sweden was neutral.

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:43.540
And in fact, a lot
of what we think

00:55:43.540 --> 00:55:45.970
of as the right-wing
movements of the 1920s--

00:55:45.970 --> 00:55:53.080
these were demobilized people
who had been in the military.

00:55:53.080 --> 00:55:56.290
They had a strong
feeling of solidarity

00:55:56.290 --> 00:55:59.100
with the people they had been
fighting in the trenches with,

00:55:59.100 --> 00:56:00.910
and a strong feeling
of having been

00:56:00.910 --> 00:56:03.500
betrayed by their government.

00:56:03.500 --> 00:56:04.750
I mean, there were countries--

00:56:04.750 --> 00:56:08.230
France and Germany lost
3 or 4 million people

00:56:08.230 --> 00:56:10.690
in the First World War.

00:56:10.690 --> 00:56:14.960
And so nationalism tended to
be claimed by those people.

00:56:14.960 --> 00:56:16.510
There was no one
claiming nationalism

00:56:16.510 --> 00:56:18.040
in the same way in Sweden.

00:56:18.040 --> 00:56:24.780
And so when the crash came--
which brought, I think,

00:56:24.780 --> 00:56:27.630
an increase in
unemployment in Sweden,

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:29.880
as it did everywhere else--

00:56:29.880 --> 00:56:34.320
there was not a ready-made
political polarity

00:56:34.320 --> 00:56:40.260
between the Marxists here
and the street-fighting

00:56:40.260 --> 00:56:42.060
paramilitaries there.

00:56:42.060 --> 00:56:44.610
There was a lot more fluidity.

00:56:44.610 --> 00:56:49.650
And I think that that made the
social democrats more capable

00:56:49.650 --> 00:56:56.040
of forming up, of making an
attractive offer to the voting

00:56:56.040 --> 00:56:58.330
public.

00:56:58.330 --> 00:57:00.963
SPEAKER: We have time
for one more question.

00:57:00.963 --> 00:57:02.380
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your talk.

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:05.190
You mentioned earlier that there
were a few prosperous companies

00:57:05.190 --> 00:57:08.155
that started in Sweden,
such as Saab and IKEA.

00:57:08.155 --> 00:57:10.530
And I was wondering, did they
start in the socialist time

00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:11.350
period?

00:57:11.350 --> 00:57:13.410
And if they did, how
were they able to thrive?

00:57:13.410 --> 00:57:13.890
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL: Right.

00:57:13.890 --> 00:57:15.120
So that's what I was--

00:57:18.060 --> 00:57:20.790
I must have been unclear,
because I mentioned

00:57:20.790 --> 00:57:23.820
the economist Tino Sanandaji.

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:28.350
And one of the things he said
is that there's a group that--

00:57:28.350 --> 00:57:32.010
actually, while
you had socialism,

00:57:32.010 --> 00:57:34.350
and while you had
entrepreneurialism,

00:57:34.350 --> 00:57:36.100
they didn't date
from the same time.

00:57:36.100 --> 00:57:37.860
They lived side by side.

00:57:37.860 --> 00:57:39.900
They weren't part
of the same system.

00:57:39.900 --> 00:57:45.030
And what he was saying is
that, let's say Electrolux,

00:57:45.030 --> 00:57:49.170
and IKEA, and H&amp;M, came out of--

00:57:49.170 --> 00:57:55.050
I think IKEA and H&amp;M were both
founded during the Second World

00:57:55.050 --> 00:58:00.750
War when the rest of Europe was
not founding companies at all.

00:58:00.750 --> 00:58:05.345
But they came before the
high tide of socialism.

00:58:05.345 --> 00:58:06.220
You know what I mean?

00:58:06.220 --> 00:58:13.110
And Spotify, and Ericsson, and
those high-tech companies--

00:58:13.110 --> 00:58:15.870
they came after the
decline of socialism.

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:20.280
So while you might
still call Sweden

00:58:20.280 --> 00:58:23.070
a social democratic
country today,

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:27.750
it's not confidently so,
or noticeably that much

00:58:27.750 --> 00:58:30.880
more so, than other
European countries.

00:58:30.880 --> 00:58:34.020
So his argument--
which I endorse--

00:58:34.020 --> 00:58:36.360
is that you had a period
of entrepreneurship,

00:58:36.360 --> 00:58:39.630
you had a period of
social democracy,

00:58:39.630 --> 00:58:42.815
and then another period
of entrepreneurship.

00:58:42.815 --> 00:58:44.190
SPEAKER: Thank
you, Mr. Caldwell.

00:58:44.190 --> 00:58:45.523
CHRISTOPHER CALDWELL: Thank you.

00:58:45.523 --> 00:58:47.480
[APPLAUSE]

